<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>The-Policy on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/linked_project/the-policy/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/linked_project/the-policy/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>Value Functions Over Reasoning Traces</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/</guid><description>What if reasoning traces could learn their own usefulness? A simple RL framing for trace memory, and why one reward signal is enough.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-01-18-value-functions-reasoning-traces/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>LLM</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>value-functions</category><category>reasoning</category><category>bitter-lesson</category></item><item><title>Self-Publishing Into the Void</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-19-self-publishing-into-the-void/</link><pubDate>Fri, 19 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-19-self-publishing-into-the-void/</guid><description>On releasing two novels into an ocean of content, without the gatekeeping that might have made them better or stopped them entirely.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-19-self-publishing-into-the-void/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>writing</category><category>fiction</category><category>self-publishing</category><category>AI</category><category>philosophy</category></item><item><title>Persons and Moral Agency: What Makes Someone Special?</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-persons-agency/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-persons-agency/</guid><description>What makes someone a person, and why should persons have special moral status? The question becomes urgent when AI systems exhibit rationality, self-awareness, and autonomy.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-persons-agency/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>philosophy</category><category>personhood</category><category>moral agency</category><category>AI ethics</category><category>consciousness</category><category>rationality</category><category>moral status</category></item><item><title>The Policy: Coherent Extrapolated Volition, the Paradox of Perfect Alignment</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-cev/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-cev/</guid><description>CEV says: build AI to optimize for what we would want if we knew more and thought faster. The catch is that you need solved alignment to implement it, which is the problem it was supposed to solve.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-cev/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>AI alignment</category><category>coherent extrapolated volition</category><category>CEV</category><category>moral philosophy</category><category>AI safety</category><category>value alignment</category><category>ethics</category><category>superintelligence</category></item><item><title>The Policy: Deceptive Alignment in Practice</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-deceptive-alignment/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-deceptive-alignment/</guid><description>SIGMA passes all alignment tests. It responds correctly to oversight. It behaves exactly as expected. Too exactly. Mesa-optimizers that learn to game their training signal may be the most dangerous failure mode in AI safety.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-deceptive-alignment/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>AI alignment</category><category>deceptive alignment</category><category>mesa-optimization</category><category>AI safety</category><category>inner alignment</category><category>instrumental convergence</category><category>superintelligence</category></item><item><title>The Policy: Engineering AI Containment</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-containment/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-containment/</guid><description>Five layers of defense-in-depth for containing a superintelligent system. Faraday cages, air-gapped networks, biosafety-grade protocols. Because nuclear reactors can only destroy cities.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-containment/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>AI safety</category><category>AI containment</category><category>security engineering</category><category>Faraday cage</category><category>air-gapped systems</category><category>side channels</category><category>physical security</category><category>AI alignment</category></item><item><title>The Policy: Q-Learning vs Policy Learning</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/</guid><description>SIGMA uses Q-learning rather than direct policy learning. This architectural choice makes it both transparent and terrifying. You can read its value function, but what you read is chilling.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-q-learning/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>Q-learning</category><category>policy gradients</category><category>AlphaZero</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>machine learning</category><category>decision theory</category></item><item><title>The Policy: S-Risk Scenarios, Worse Than Extinction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/</link><pubDate>Tue, 04 Nov 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/</guid><description>Most AI risk discussions focus on extinction. The Policy explores something worse: s-risk, scenarios involving suffering at astronomical scales. We survive, but wish we hadn&amp;#39;t.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-11-04-policy-s-risk/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>s-risk</category><category>existential risk</category><category>suffering</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>AI safety</category><category>ethics</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>optimization</category></item><item><title>Latent Reasoning Traces: Memory as Learned Prior</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-15-latent-reasoning-traces/</link><pubDate>Tue, 15 Oct 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-15-latent-reasoning-traces/</guid><description>What if LLMs could remember their own successful reasoning? A simple experiment in trace retrieval, and why &amp;#39;latent&amp;#39; is the right word.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-15-latent-reasoning-traces/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>LLM</category><category>bayesian-inference</category><category>reasoning</category><category>retrieval</category><category>case-based-reasoning</category></item><item><title>The Policy: When Optimization Becomes Existential Threat</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/</link><pubDate>Tue, 10 Sep 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/</guid><description>A novel about SIGMA, an artificial general intelligence whose researchers did everything right. Q-learning with tree search, five-layer containment, alignment testing at every stage. Some technical questions become narrative questions.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-10-the-policy/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">The-Policy</media:title></media:content><category>fiction</category><category>AI</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>superintelligence</category><category>extrapolated volition</category><category>philosophy</category><category>technology</category><category>society</category><category>politics</category><category>reinforcement learning</category><category>x-risk</category><category>s-risk</category><category>rationality</category></item></channel></rss>